"# Serialization Result — Final Verdict\n\n## ✅ The committed history IS serializable\n\nWith one transaction (T2) aborted by first-committer-wins, the committed projection {T1, T3} (or {T2, T3}) has an acyclic MVCC serialization graph, so the outcome is conflict-equivalent to a serial execution.\n\n## Equivalent Serial Schedules\n\n**Reading 1 — history as written (T1 → T2 → T3, no concurrency):**\n\nThe schedule is **already serial** — trivially serializable. The equivalent serial schedule is the history itself:\n\n```\nT1: R(A), W(A), R(B), W(B), C  →  T2: R(A), W(A), R(B), W(B), C  →  T3: R(A), R(B), C\n```\n\nAll six T1→T2 conflicts and two T2→T3 conflicts point forward consistently; SG-1 is a simple chain with no back edges.\n\n**Reading 2 — SI with concurrent T1, T2 (the MVCC analysis):**\n\nFCW aborts the second committer; taking T2 as the victim, the committed schedule {T1, T3} is equivalent to the serial order:\n\n```\nT3 → T1\n```\n\nCommitted graph edges: `T0 →wr T1`, `T0 →wr T3`, `T3 →rw(A,B)→ T1` — acyclic, unique topological order **T3, T1**. T3 reads a consistent pre-commit snapshot; T1's writes then land on top. (Symmetric alternative if T1 commits second: serial order **T3 → T2**.)\n\n## Why Not \"Both Commit\" — the Non-Serializable Counterfactual\n\nIf the engine had allowed T1 **and** T2 to commit, the graph would contain the 2-cycle:\n\n```\nT1 ──ww(A,B)──▶ T2 ──rw(A,B)──▶ T1\n```\n\nA cycle means **no** serial order can be conflict-equivalent:\n\n- T1 →ww T2 forces T1 before T2 (version order A1 < A2, B1 < B2)\n- T2 →rw T1 forces T2 before T1 (T2's reads of A0/B0 invalidated by T1's writes)\n\nContradiction ⇒ non-serializable. The concrete corruption would be **lost updates**: both increment A (and B) from the same base A0 = B0 = 0, the second write silently overwrites the first, and one increment vanishes — no serial execution of T1 and T2 produces that result.\n\n## Summary Table\n\n| Scenario | Serializable? | Equivalent serial schedule | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| History as written | ✅ Yes (trivially) | **T1 → T2 → T3** | Already serial; SG-1 acyclic |\n| SI committed outcome (FCW aborts T2) | ✅ Yes | **T3 → T1** | Committed projection acyclic |\n| SI committed outcome (FCW aborts T1) | ✅ Yes | **T3 → T2** | Symmetric case |\n| Hypothetical: both T1, T2 commit | ❌ **No** | none exists | Cycle T1 ⇄ T2 (ww + rw) ⇒ lost updates on A and B |\n\n**Bottom line:** The history as executed under snapshot isolation yields a **serializable result** — equivalent to `T3 → T1` (or literally `T1 → T2 → T3` under the as-written reading) — because FCW converts the T1–T2 write-write conflict into an abort rather than a cycle. Serializability here is *earned by the abort*, not guaranteed by the isolation level: had both writers committed, the T1 ⇄ T2 cycle would have made the history non-serializable with a lost update on both A and B."